this machine was destined for russia but  under current conditions it won't be  delivered  its export has been prohibited for fear  it could be used for military purposes  the machine is used to produce parts for  trucks  but as russian customers could also use  it to produce military equipment  the german company that manufactures it  is not allowed to ship it  and this is just the beginning  we haven't had any orders at all  recently we're still in negotiations  with our russian partners on several  projects but of course it's extremely  difficult  the company based in saxony used to sell  about a quarter of all its machines to  russia  the export ban is also a big problem for  russian firms  they have no idea whether they'll be  able to get the machines they need  whether their german suppliers will be  allowed to deliver them  it throws up very important questions  concerning reliability  our russian customers simply can't  depend on us at the moment  the region around chemnitz is an  important center of mechanical  engineering  when it was part of former east germany  it delivered supplies to companies in  the soviet union  the good business relationships with the  former soviet states continued after the  fall of the berlin wall  this producer of drilling machines has  obtained permission to supply one of its  russian customers after he obtained a  special export license  an additional problem is that nowadays  in russia there are a lot of big  companies with several subsidiaries and  in many cases one or the other of these  subsidiaries makes military equipment  proving that isn't actually the case  with particular companies has become  extremely difficult  these machines are used to produce  electric engines  their use is unambiguous  despite that the company has received no  new orders for them  russian customers are holding back  at the moment we're not in a position to  cultivate those business relationships  so that they develop into long-term  commitments  we hope the situation doesn't last too  long  then perhaps we'll be able to repair our  damaged customer relations  but if it goes on for a long time and  our customers manage to find new supply  channels that suit them it'll be  difficult to get them back  many jobs in chemnitz depend on the  success of the mechanical engineering  sector so local people are following  political developments between the eu  and russia with great concern  a survey done by germany's chamber of  industry and commerce confirms that one  in ten companies is directly affected by  the economic sanctions  it will be reflected in job losses and  difficult situations for businesses  some companies have already applied for  government schemes which allow them to  reduce working hours  other businesses have applied for state  bank guarantees to help them get  bridging loans the highest  that shows us this is not something that  could be a problem in the future  it's a crisis right here and now  as long as the sanctions remain in place  the situation will get worse  the biggest worry of many companies is  that the eu trade sanctions could be  made even more comprehensive  if that happens it'll be almost  impossible for them to sell any machines  to russian customers at all
 

